$CATEGORY: iso-iec-27005

::Note ahead of the following questions::
The following questions test the order, meaning the risk treatment first and the
comparison against the annex after it, and whether a reversed order is
recognised in the result. References are by standard, clause and edition.

// 1

::The order::
The controls are determined out of the risk treatment, and the comparison
against the annex comes after that.{TRUE
####Correct. ISO/IEC 27001\:2022 asks for that order in 6.1.3, and the
comparison is a check for what was forgotten there and not a starting point.}

// 2

::The clause that asks for the assessment::
Which clause of ISO/IEC 27001\:2022 asks for the risk assessment?{
=6.1.2 #Correct.
~6.1.3 #That is the treatment, which builds on the assessment.
~8.2 #That is carrying out the assessment in operation.
~9.1 #That is the measurement.
####6.1.2 and 6.1.3 are two steps in that order. 8.2 and 8.3 ask that they
actually take place in operation.}

// 3

::What starting from the annex leads to::
An organisation goes through the numbers of the annex from the front and decides
per number whether it is applied. What follows from that?{
=Controls come into being with no risk behind them, and the justification is
missing. #Correct.
~The same selection comes out as through the risk treatment, only faster. #Then
the order would not matter, and it does.
~It is admissible as long as a statement of applicability stands at the end.
#The statement is the result of the treatment and not a substitute for it.
~It saves the assessment. #The assessment is asked for in 6.1.2 and cannot be
deselected.
####The annex does not know this organisation's situation. Read from the front
it supplies a list, read from the back it shows what was missed.}

// 4

::Signs of the reversed order::
Which two observations point at the annex having been the starting point?{
~%50%The statement of applicability is full, the risk register is thin.
#Correct.
~%50%For an applied control, no row in the register can be named. #Correct.
~%-100%The number of controls that are not applied is high. #That can be a
cleanly justified selection.
~%-100%The register carries rows with no control. #A carried risk is an
admissible decision.
####Both signs are gaps between the register and the statement. The other two
observations are unremarkable on their own.}

// 5

::The parts of the assessment and the step after it::
Match each step to what comes into being in it.{
=Identifying -> the row in the register
=Analysing -> the numbers for likelihood and consequence
=Evaluating -> the decision whether the risk may stay as it is
=Treating -> the controls and the residual risk
####The first three belong to the assessment under 6.1.2, the fourth is the step
after it under 6.1.3.}

// 6

::What is left over::
What is the thing called that is left of a risk once the controls work?{
=Residual risk
=residual risk
=the residual risk
####It is never zero. Anyone who thinks it is has overestimated the controls,
and it is approved expressly, by somebody who can carry the consequence.}

// 7

::A row with no owner::
A risk register carries a row with nobody entered as its owner. What follows?{
=The row is administered and not treated, because nobody decides about it.
#Correct.
~The row is thereby carried automatically. #A carried risk is a decision, and a
decision needs somebody to make it.
~The row belongs to the management. #Only if it is entered there.
~The row is invalid and is struck out. #The risk does not go away because the
row goes away.
####A decision with nobody to make it is none. The owner is the difference
between a register and a collection.}

// 8

::Gap, the clause of the treatment::
The risk treatment and the comparison against the annex stand in ISO/IEC
27001\:2022 in {=6.1.3 ~6.1.2 ~6.2 ~8.3}.{####6.1.2 is the assessment before it,
6.2 are the objectives, and 8.3 asks for the treatment to be carried out in
operation.}

// 9

::The threshold::
Where does the threshold come from above which a risk is treated?{
=From the organisation, which fixes it beforehand. #Correct.
~From ISO/IEC 27005. #The guidance names no threshold.
~From the annex of ISO/IEC 27001\:2022. #That carries controls and no
thresholds.
~From the certification body. #It checks that a criterion exists and does not
set it.
####The criterion is fixed before the evaluation. Anyone fixing it afterwards
fixes it so the result fits.}

// 10

::Number of ways::
On how many ways is an evaluated risk treated in this training?{#4:0
####Make it smaller, drop it, share it, carry it. All four are decisions, and
all four are justified.}

// 11

::What the comparison does::
The comparison against the annex supplies controls that were not determined in
the treatment.{TRUE
####Correct, and that is exactly why it stands at the back. It finds what was
forgotten; it invents no need, because taking in a number that was found stays
tied to a row of the register.}

// 12

::A justification that is none::
In a statement of applicability, all that stands for a control that is not
applied is\: "too expensive". Write in two sentences what is missing and what has
to stand there instead.{
####Model answer. What is missing is the pointer back at the risk treatment\:
which risk the control would treat and why that risk is treated another way or
carried. Cost alone is no justification as long as no risk is named for it, and
the row of the register belongs with it.}
